Test rg32: constants-pool determinism (no ASLR sensitivity)
// Two plain runs of this file (NO setarch -R) must produce byte-identical
// output -- run_regression.sh's rg32 block assembles it twice and compares.
// Before 28-Aug-26, constant fingerprints for unresolved symbols came from
// the symbol node's malloc ADDRESS, so the constants-pool layout followed
// the allocator under ASLR and every run gave a different binary. Now:
// unresolved symbol refs use a creation-order serial (sym_make), and
// wildcard refs digest the NAME (FNV-1a) -- both identical run to run.
// The stored .ref additionally pins the exact layout, including:
//   - an unresolved-at-use forward ref deduping to one slot ([fwd] twice)
//   - a distinct expression on the same symbol getting its own slot
//   - wildcard refs deduping BY NAME ([wsym:*] twice -> one slot; the old
//     string-pointer hashing never deduped these at all)
// If the fingerprint scheme is ever deliberately changed, regenerate the
// .ref (am1 -T, copy the .dmp) -- layout pinning is the point, not a law.
100/
    lac [fwd            // forward ref, unresolved when this constant is hashed
    lio [fwd            // same symbol -- must share one pool slot
    lac [fwd+1          // distinct expression on the same symbol -- own slot
    lio [wsym:*         // wildcard by name, unresolved
    lac [wsym:*         // same name -- must share one pool slot
    lac [17
    lio [17             // plain value dedup, sanity
    hlt
fwd, 0
    constants

bank 1
200/
wsym, 0
b1fwd, 0
    lac [b1fwd          // per-bank pool, its own forward-ref slot
    hlt
    constants

start 100
